<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Natsume called this morning and was seen the same day. Two days ago he lifted a heavy box of books from the floor of his study and felt a pull across his lower back. The pain is a dull ache across both sides of the low back, worse when he bends or gets up from the floor, and eased by lying on his side. It does not spread down his legs. He has no numbness, weakness, or trouble passing urine or stool, no fever, and no history of cancer. He wanted to take the ibuprofen he used to keep for headaches but remembered that he must not, and asks what he may take instead. He is on day eight of fourteen of his eradication therapy, taking every dose; he has slightly loose stools once a day and a metallic taste but no diarrhea, rash or abdominal pain. His stomach pain has gone, and his stools are dark from the iron but formed. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, lifting a box of books</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Low back, both sides; no radiation to the legs</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Moderate; 5 out of 10 on bending</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Worse with bending and rising; eased lying on his side; has taken no NSAID</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Gastric ulcer on vonoprazan; day 8 of 14 of vonoprazan-amoxicillin dual therapy</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No leg numbness or weakness, bladder or bowel change, fever, or weight loss</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

