<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute low back strain (M54.50)</content>. Mechanical low back strain with no red flags: no neurological signs, no fever, no history of cancer, and a clear lifting injury. No imaging is needed. NSAIDs, usually first-line, must be avoided: they caused or worsen peptic ulcers and could provoke bleeding from the healing gastric ulcer. [S]<list><item>Stay active; superficial heat for 20 minutes several times a day.</item><item>Acetaminophen 500 mg to 1 g every six hours as needed, no more than 3 g a day, for a few days.</item><item>No ibuprofen, naproxen, aspirin or other NSAID, including combination cold and headache remedies.</item><item>Lift from the knees, or have books moved for him.</item><item>Return for leg weakness, saddle numbness, bladder or bowel change, fever, or pain not easing in four weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Helicobacter pylori eradication therapy, day 8 of 14 (B96.81)</content>. Taking every dose; the loose stools and metallic taste are expected side effects of amoxicillin with vonoprazan and are not a reason to stop. No diarrhea, so no stool testing is needed. [S]<list><item>Finish all 14 days.</item><item>Call for watery diarrhea three or more times a day, blood in the stool, rash or abdominal pain.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Gastric ulcer, healing (K25.4)</content>. Epigastric pain gone on vonoprazan. Iron makes the stools dark but formed; black, sticky, tarry stools or vomiting blood would mean bleeding. [S]<list><item>Call 119 for tarry stools, vomiting blood or coffee-ground material, or fainting.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: ACG Clinical Guideline: Treatment of Helicobacter pylori Infection (2024); Evidence-based clinical practice guidelines for peptic ulcer disease 2026 (2026); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit in five days as booked; eleven-week review as planned. Return sooner for any warning sign. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-16: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
